{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'gates|closure|projection|float' collab/yang-base/1795196337796-architect-yang-preludes-design-r3.gpt-6.2-astra.findings.md | head -20\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'gates|closure|projection|float' collab/yang-base/1795196337796-architect-yang-preludes-design-r3.gpt-6.2-astra.findings.md | head -20\"","aggregated_output":"15:**Five r2 findings are `CONFIRMED-FIXED`. F1, F3, and F4 remain `OPEN`.** The value-returning exception boundary, cell-backed closure construction, vector frame access, boxed float status interface, and active effect-ceiling example each resolve the specific defect reported in r2.\n17:The design still permits an integer limit result to become a host failure, specifies a float floor-division algorithm that returns the wrong integer for ordinary finite inputs, and leaves the path from a published library export to an invocable task-local callable incomplete. These affect P1 and P3 acceptance.\n19:Session ID: `01a1fa0a-94cf-7df3-acd0-16353342ed04`. This was a read-only design and source review. I checked the floor-division and power witnesses with in-memory Python arithmetic. No files were edited. Repository suites and four-host acceptance gates were not run.\n25:| F1 | Portable numeric classification, guarded dispatch, and collection equality | **OPEN** | The classifier now recognizes boxed CLJS floats. Negative integer powers retain an exact exponent magnitude, and `%` guards the `fmod-pos` domain. The finite `//` algorithm still misrounds. Float exponent conversion can pass `::bit-limit` to `integer/compare-float`. Recursive equality also leaves key insertion and lookup dependent on host equality. [Numeric dispatch](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:528) · [Collection equality](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:554) |\n29:| F5 | Dynamic closure construction and internal higher-order calls | **CONFIRMED-FIXED** | The construction rule explicitly covers closures created during `comp` and `partial`. It preserves the returned cell’s identity. Static sibling calls use internal lambdas, while dynamic callback calls use the public invocation convention. The separate linker projection and provenance-ownership issues below concern how that contract is reached. [Construction and dispatch](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:633) |\n41:| **#9 — Recipe/profile separation and verified closure** | **The r2 correction remains in place.** The resolved profile pins the owning root and the design requires verified dependency manifests. Admission still needs an explicit source for the installed numeric limits, and descriptor targets must resolve against the selected closure. [Profile and closure](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:266) |\n42:| **#10 — Roadmap and acceptance gates** | **Phase boundaries remain fixed. A P1 gate has regressed.** P0 and P2 retain byte-parity requirements, P3 preserves default Clojure bundling, and D4 requires independent validation before activation. P1 now lists a successful zero result as its exceptional underflow witness. [Roadmap](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:695) |\n48:| **HIGH** | [yang.preludes.md:541](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:541) | For `2.1 // 0.7`, the specified intermediate quotient is `2.9999999999999996`. Flooring gives `2.0`. The floored quotient is `3.0`. The live Python helper corrects the rounded quotient and separately preserves the sign of a zero quotient. The proposed exact-integer floor also loses that sign. [Live divmod calculation](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:1425) | Specify the finite quotient correction and signed-zero branch. Pin `2.1 // 0.7` and `-0.0 // 2.0`. Also state the float/mixed `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, and unary adapter rules, including the `/` zero-divisor policy. §7.2 names those operations but gives detailed float dispatch only for `//`, `%`, `**`, and ordering. |\n49:| **HIGH** | [yang.preludes.md:549](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:549), [line 624](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:624) | At the admitted 53-bit floor, the finite float exponent `2.0^53` requires a 54-bit integer. `integer/from-float` can return `::bit-limit`, which the proposed expression passes to `integer/compare-float`. Likewise, `quot-rem` returns either a pair or a reason, and `rem` applies `data/nth` first. Both routes can turn a guest resource refusal into a host wrong-type failure. [Float conversion](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc:454) · [Pair contract](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc:178) | Unwrap boxed float inputs at the leaf, classify the conversion result before comparison, and translate the status after leaving the raw frame. Check `quot-rem` before indexing. Apply the same rule to `integer/neg` on externally supplied exact operands. Test at the minimum admitted limit. |\n50:| **HIGH** | [yang.preludes.md:561](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:561), [line 632](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:632) | Recursive comparison cannot recover keys or elements already collapsed by host collection construction. Normalizing a top-level `-0.0` misses a vector key containing it. Projected `assoc`, `get`, `conj`, and retained `contains?` also bypass the stated normalization. Jing explicitly refuses certain equal-value, different-kind key collisions. [Data-module key contract](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/data.cljc:34) · [Jing collection contract](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:173) | Define one portable key/element domain for construction, update, lookup, and membership, including nested keys, NaNs, and equal-valued integer/float keys. A v1 restriction with an explicit guest refusal is sufficient. Test literal construction followed by projected update and lookup on every host. |\n51:| **HIGH** | [yang.preludes.md:594](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:594), [line 628](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:628) | The common emitter recognizes `if`, `let`, and a small set of other forms. It emits `and` as an application, and `and` has no standard VM primitive binding. The displayed predicate therefore cannot provide the required short circuit as written. Separately, `data/callable?` includes host functions and continuations. The optional `fn?` branch can report true for a value the standalone higher-order dispatcher refuses. [Emitter](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/core/uast.cljc:82) · [VM primitives](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:396) · [Applicability predicate](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/data.cljc:456) | Emit nested `if` nodes or explicitly expand short-circuit syntax before emission. Use the same source admission predicate in `fn?`, ordinary calls, and higher-order calls. Keep raw implementation closures on the private direct-call path. |\n55:| **HIGH** | [yang.preludes.md:621](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:621) | The retained “non-numeric” surface still includes `fresh-symbol`, which increments the caller’s `:counter` with bare `+`. Projected `str` delegates to host `str`, so boxed `1.0` can render as `\"1\"` on JS and `\"1.0\"` on the JVM. `keys` and `vals` expose host map iteration order as an ordered sequence. [Counter and string definitions](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2689) · [JS carrier formatting](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/host/number.cljc:76) · [Keys and values](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2861) | Audit the retained exports by observable operation. Use the integer kernel for caller-owned counters, specify portable string rendering and map traversal, or narrow the exported domains. Include these exports in the four-host gate. |\n56:| **HIGH** | [yang.preludes.md:379](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:379), [line 466](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:466) | `:raw-or-call` carries an executable `:overflow-op`. The exact-export and reserved-door checks enumerate `:hooks` and `:ambient-projections`, leaving this literal-strategy target outside the stated checks. Finding an export somewhere in `artifact-store` also does not establish that the selected runtime closure authorizes it for that position. | Validate every descriptor field that can emit a call against the selected, address-verified closure and its frontend-authorized role. Apply qualification, arity, and reserved-door rules to literal strategies as well as hooks and projections. |\n57:| **MEDIUM** | [yang.preludes.md:302](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:302) | The proposed validator promises to check numeric floors from the registry. Host registration publishes export profiles and functions. `integer/max-bits` is absent from the export inventory, and the existing Python registrar checks the explicit limits map before constructing the registry. [Host registration](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:249) · [Integer exports](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc:592) · [Python registration](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:4163) | Pass the trusted composition limits into admission or retain verifiable limit declarations at registration. Distinguish the root’s direct `:requires` pins from the transitively verified closure so a nested dependency need not be declared as a direct edge. |\n58:| **MEDIUM** | [yang.preludes.md:698](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:698), [line 182](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:182) | `2.0 ** -2000` returns boxed `+0.0` on the existing split-reciprocal path. The exceptional status applies when the *positive power in the denominator* rounds to zero. The dependency list also requires `data/float64?` for `yrt.float`, while §7.2 makes its installation optional in P1. [Existing negative-power algorithm](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:1530) · [Prerequisite schedule](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:524) | Keep `2.0 ** -2000` as a normal-zero witness. Use `0.5 ** -2000` for the underflow status and outer overflow policy, and keep `2.0 ** -1074` for the nonzero subnormal path. Put the new predicate in the first phase that actually requires it. |\n60:| **LOW** | [yang.preludes.md:283](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:283) | The active `nil` ceiling works. The adjacent four-effect example omits at least `:cell/new-provenance` and `:cell/provenance`, both used by the Python runtime. [Python host dependencies](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:204) · [Declared effects](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:446) | Remove the illustrative set or derive a sufficient example from the verified Python closure. |\n68:| **D3 — Python float tags** | Concur with **A**. Retain `{:py/float ...}` during P1 to preserve Python’s observable representation across extraction. |\n74:D1–D7 remain open for owner disposition. Architectural concurrence records a recommendation. The `REVISE` verdict concerns the concrete contracts and acceptance gates above.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
