{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc | sed -n '1,108p;576,622p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t(ns yin.vm.integer\n     2\t  \"The `integer` host module: exact-integer kernels a language prelude\n     3\t   writes its integer semantics over (C3 big-integer design, converged\n     4\t   rulings 1, 2, 5 and 11).\n     5\t\n     6\t   Every export is `:pure` with declared effects `#{}`. As with\n     7\t   `yin.vm.data`, the module is not part of `yin.vm/primitives`: a\n     8\t   composition whose runtime profile names it installs it explicitly with\n     9\t   `register-integer-module`, and Yin source reaches the exports as\n    10\t   `integer/add`, `integer/parse` and so on. Guest integers go through\n    11\t   these exports only; a VM's internal counters stay VM primitives.\n    12\t\n    13\t   Carriers. Every argument may be any exact-integer carrier the host has\n    14\t   (`dao.host.integer`); every integer result is in its one canonical\n    15\t   carrier: native iff within signed 64 bits on the JVM and Dart, or\n    16\t   within +/-(2^53 - 1) on JS, else the host big integer. Each kernel\n    17\t   promotes its operands to the host big integer before it operates and\n    18\t   demotes the result after, so no result ever passes through a rounded\n    19\t   or wrapped native intermediate, and equal values are `=` on every\n    20\t   host. The kernels are written once here over `dao.host.integer`;\n    21\t   the float and float-text arithmetic (`ratio->float`, `trunc-big`,\n    22\t   `float-digits`, `decimal->float`) is `dao.host.float`'s, and this\n    23\t   module keeps only the argument checks and refusals. Under a\n    24\t   `::max-bits` of 53 or more, `add`, `sub`, `mul` and `compare` first\n    25\t   try a native fast path on operands within +/-(2^53 - 1) whose result\n    26\t   is provably exact, and fall back to the big path otherwise.\n    27\t\n    28\t   Semantics, identical on every host:\n    29\t\n    30\t   - `add`, `sub`, `mul`, `neg` and `compare` are exact; `compare`\n    31\t     answers -1, 0 or 1.\n    32\t   - `quot-rem` answers `[q r]` truncated toward zero, `a = b*q + r`;\n    33\t     `floor-div-mod` answers Python's `[a // b, a % b]`: the quotient\n    34\t     rounds toward negative infinity and a non-zero remainder takes the\n    35\t     divisor's sign.\n    36\t   - `bit-and`, `bit-or`, `bit-xor` and `bit-not` act on infinite signed\n    37\t     two's complement, so `(bit-not a)` is `-a - 1`. `shift-left` is\n    38\t     `a * 2^k` and `shift-right` is `floor(a / 2^k)`; the count is any\n    39\t     exact integer and is never narrowed before it is checked, and a\n    40\t     right shift past the operand's length answers 0 or -1 without\n    41\t     building `2^k`.\n    42\t   - `bit-length` is the bit length of the absolute value, 0 for zero.\n    43\t   - The float exports (version 3) take and answer host binary64s: a JVM\n    44\t     `Double`, a Dart `double`, a JS `Number`. `to-float` is CPython's\n    45\t     `float(int)`, nearest with ties to even; `true-div` is CPython's\n    46\t     `int / int`, the exact ratio correctly rounded however large both\n    47\t     operands are, subnormal results included and a zero quotient\n    48\t     signed by the operands; a result that is not finite answers\n    49\t     `::float-overflow`. `compare-float` compares an integer with a\n    50\t     float exactly, -1, 0 or 1, never rounding the integer: the caller\n    51\t     excludes NaN, and an infinity compares by its sign. `from-float`\n    52\t     truncates a finite float toward zero, exactly. Each is one\n    53\t     algorithm over the shim, never a host conversion of a big integer.\n    54\t   - The float text exports (version 4) use no host float parser or\n    55\t     printer. `float-digits` answers `[digits exp10]` for a finite\n    56\t     nonzero float `v`: `digits` is the shortest decimal string, no\n    57\t     leading or trailing zero, that reads back to `v`, the closest to\n    58\t     `v` among the shortest (CPython's `repr` digits), and |v| is\n    59\t     d1.d2...dn x 10^exp10; zero, NaN and infinity are the caller's to\n    60\t     exclude. `decimal->float` is CPython's `float(str)` after syntax:\n    61\t     `digits x 10^exp10` for one or more ASCII digits and any exact\n    62\t     integer exponent, rounded once to nearest with ties to even and\n    63\t     non-negative; it answers `+inf` past the range and `0.0` below it,\n    64\t     never a limit reason, and `::max-digits` never applies to its text.\n    65\t     `max-digits` answers the composition's `::max-digits`.\n    66\t   - `pow` raises to a non-negative exponent exactly, by squaring.\n    67\t   - `parse` reads an optional `-` and one or more digits of `radix` (2\n    68\t     to 36, default 10, either letter case) exactly, never through a\n    69\t     float or a native-width parser. Signs other than `-`, prefixes,\n    70\t     underscores and whitespace are the prelude's syntax, not this\n    71\t     kernel's. `format` writes exact lowercase text, a `-` only when\n    72\t     negative, no prefix.\n    73\t\n    74\t   Limits (ruling 11) are explicit composition data with no default, in\n    75\t   bits and digits: `::max-bits` bounds the bit length of every integer\n    76\t   result, and `::max-digits` bounds the digit count `parse` reads and\n    77\t   `format` writes in a radix that is not a power of two. A result that\n    78\t   would breach `::max-bits` is refused before it is built wherever its\n    79\t   size is known in advance (`mul`, `shift-left`, `pow`).\n    80\t\n    81\t   A limit breach is returned, not thrown (version 2): the call answers\n    82\t   the keyword `::bit-limit` or `::digit-limit` in place of its result,\n    83\t   and a float result that is not finite answers `::float-overflow`\n    84\t   (version 3). Every other result is an integer, a float, a boolean, a\n    85\t   string or a pair, so the sum needs no tag, and a pair is never\n    86\t   answered with one half refused. A guest prelude translates the three\n    87\t   reasons into catchable guest exceptions; the Python profile maps\n    88\t   `::bit-limit` to `MemoryError` and `::digit-limit` to `ValueError`,\n    89\t   never `OverflowError` (`py/int-result`), and its translator must\n    90\t   learn `::float-overflow`, which is CPython's `OverflowError`, \\\"int too\n    91\t   large to convert to float\\\".\n    92\t\n    93\t   Every other refusal throws `ex-info` with the message\n    94\t   `refusal-message` and ex-data built here, never host exception text,\n    95\t   never an operand: `::op`, `::reason` and the reason's own keys. These\n    96\t   reasons are `:arity`, `:wrong-type`, `:out-of-range`, `:syntax`,\n    97\t   `:zero-division`, `:negative-count` and `:negative-exponent`: a\n    98\t   caller checks for them before the call, so reaching one is a defect\n    99\t   of the caller, and it stays a host failure, never a guest\n   100\t   exception.\"\n   101\t  (:require\n   102\t    [clojure.string :as str]\n   103\t    [dao.host.float :as float-kernel]\n   104\t    [dao.host.integer :as host]\n   105\t    [yin.vm :as vm]\n   106\t    [yin.vm.module :as module]))\n   107\t\n   108\t\n   576\t;; =============================================================================\n   577\t\n   578\t(defn- int-integer?\n   579\t  [_limits x]\n   580\t  (host/exact-integer? x))\n   581\t\n   582\t\n   583\t(defn- int-normalize\n   584\t  [limits a]\n   585\t  (out limits (big! 'normalize 0 a)))\n   586\t\n   587\t\n   588\t;; =============================================================================\n   589\t;; The module\n   590\t;; =============================================================================\n   591\t\n   592\t(def ^:private exports\n   593\t  \"`[name arities kernel]` of every export.